course: dictionary
lesson: 5134
title: Investment in context
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: An investment is money you put into something, hoping to get more back later. Look at the words around it to find its meaning. «Many crypto scams promise huge returns: turning £10 into £1,000 in a month means 100 times more, and no real investment can promise that.» The clues are huge returns and promise. A real investment grows slowly and carefully, but a scam promises fast riches.
explain: Investment can also mean time or effort. «Productivity is how much is made per worker; investment is spending now to get more later.» Spending now means giving something up today, and more later means a benefit in the future. So the word can describe spending on the future, not only spending money.
explain: Watch the phrases. «Those new shoes were a good investment» means the shoes will last and be useful for a long time, so they are worth the cost. A politician may say «investment» and mean government spending. Check the context, then try swapping in "spending" to see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: An investment is money put into something, like a business, shares or property, hoping to get more back later.
fact: «Investment» can also mean time, effort or money spent on something useful later, like studying.
fact: The thing you put money into can also be called an investment.
fact: Useful phrases: «a good investment», «a wise investment», «return on investment», «investment in».
fact: In politics, «investment» often just means government spending, such as «an investment in our schools».
exercise: «Many crypto scams promise huge returns, and no real investment can promise that.» What does a real investment suggest? | A stolen wallet | *Money that is guaranteed to double in a month | Money put in carefully that may grow slowly, not a promise of fast riches | Free money handed out to everyone
exercise: «Productivity is how much is made per worker; investment is spending now to get more later.» In this sentence, what is an investment? | *Spending now to get more later | Making a worker work faster today only | Paying the worker less later | Money given away for free
exercise: Mum says, «Those new shoes were a good investment, they will last for years.» What does good investment mean here? | They were very cheap and bought in a sale | They were given to her for free | *Worth the cost because they will be useful for a long time | Shoes that are too small
test: Dad says, «Learning to code is a wise investment for your future.» What does wise investment suggest? | A holiday that ends quickly | Money that will be lost for ever | *Time and effort spent now that will be useful later | A shop that sells computers only
test: A politician says, «This is an investment in our schools.» What might it mean? | A school that sells shoes | Money left in a bank for ever | *Government spending on schools | A school trip paid for by parents only
exam: Jayden says investment only means money in a bank. What is the best reply? | Yes, only banks use investment | Yes, but only in maths lessons | No, it means shoes only | *No, it can also mean time or effort spent on something useful later
